WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Sparse Neuron sets in FrozenBirt enable efficient ai text detection across generators with 86-94% retained accuracy. This is Models &amp; Agents Episode 187. Researchers mapped under 1% of Neurons in a FrozenBirt-based Uncased model that drive ai text detection on the RAID benchmark. The selected Neurons retain most accuracy when used alone. They flip predictions in order of magnitude more often than random sets.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.720
under bidirectional patching.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.360
A separate production study at Spotify shows a synthetic data pipeline

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:42.080
plus self-improvement loop raised conversational recommendation quality by 8%.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.300
It delivered 14% more user listening in live AB tests.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:54.100
The study applied L1 to L2 sparse probing to all 9,216 CLS hidden state dimensions

00:00:54.100 --> 00:01:01.740
across 12 layers of a frozen B.E.R.T. dash-based, dash-uncased encoder on the R.A.I.D. benchmark.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:09.680
It recovered a stable set of under 1% of neurons per generator that remained consistent across folds and seeds.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.780
A probe restricted to that set retained most of the full feature detection accuracy.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:22.140
By directional activation patching, flipped predictions in order of magnitude more often than size-matched random sets.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.420
mean ablating the same neurons left accuracy largely intact.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:32.880
Instruction-tune generators concentrated 30-36% of stable neurons in the final layer,

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.380
while base generators stayed below 14%.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:44.920
Leave one family out evaluation showed the neurons retained 86-94% of full-feature ceiling on unseen generator families.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.300
The architecture replaces attention with a stack of autoencoder-based mixing modules

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:55.340
operating over local neighborhoods, the full sequence and across attention heads.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:02.200
Each module compresses and reconstructs its input through a low-rank bottleneck whose width is a hyperparameter.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.660
An iterative refinement procedure in masked positions first pulls an embedding toward a weighted average of neighbors,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.640
then projects the result back to the learned manifold.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.500
When pre-trained on C4 and compared with parameter-matched BERT baselines,

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.980
fans, the model reached a significant portion of attention performance at roughly 1.9 times

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.420
fewer FLOPs.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:29.860
A frequency-aware training schedule that oversamples rare tokens allowed the model to equal parameter

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:34.120
matched BERT and tiny BERT baselines on the rarest token frequency bucket.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:41.720
The survey organizes 211 studies published from 2018 to early 2026 by evidence source and

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:42.440
fusion level.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:48.980
It covers review text, sentiment, rating behavior, temporal metadata, user product graphs,

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.740
multimodal content, external knowledge, and LLM generated signals.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.600
Performance trends are reported on Amazon, Yelp, and OpSPAM benchmark families, while

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.620
noting limitations from differing label construction, data splits, and evaluation protocols.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.840
Slide Lab is a training-free multi-agent framework that plans a presentation narrative, then

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.460
One builds and iteratively refines a shared slide deck using agents for content planning,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.580
visual generation, layout refinement, and grounding verification.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.800
In a blind human preference study, it was preferred over both open source and commercial

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:30.860
systems on 77% of papers, while using roughly four times fewer inference tokens than the

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.480
strongest open source baseline.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:39.220
CardiGraph is a federated MCP proxy that reduces agent visible tool discovery from O of

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.820
and catalog traversal to O of K progressive disclosure.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:49.720
It combines operator attested L25519 signed capability cards, a three-layer,

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.260
confuseable cluster analysis called RIFT, and two-stage retrieval that ranks

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.540
servers before tools.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:02.220
On a 22 server, 374 tool deployment, it exposes three proxy tools instead of

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.880
374 definitions.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:13.280
A 49-queery benchmark yields R at 5 of 0.816 versus 0.592 for a jack-yard keyword baseline.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:20.920
A measured top 5 discovery exchange uses 475 tokens rather than 42,450.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:28.600
Rift identifies 49 confusible clusters including four high-risk clusters in bootstrap generated cards.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:36.740
Gateway measurements add 5 milliseconds mean latency, or 0.8% relative to direct STDIO

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:37.680
MCP calls.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.880
High-comer maintains validity of team and individual memories, then retrieves only currently valid

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.200
memories rather than ranking a flat pool by semantic relevance or recency.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:54.460
It consists of a hierarchical memory conflict updater, a validity-aware memory retriever,

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.760
and a memory-grounded answer generator.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:02.340
Two new data sets were constructed for memory-grounded question answering in collaborative settings.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:08.760
Experiments show high-comer consistently outperformed strong baselines by reducing outdated retrieval,

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:13.260
preserving current team consensus and improving downstream QA quality.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:20.520
Signed Trace integrates LLM-based dictionary enrichment, action extraction, dictionary-style rewriting,

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:27.820
7 channel retrieval and candidate re-ranking over 6,699 Chinese Sign Language Dictionary

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:33.760
entries. On a dictionary-derived benchmark of 500 movement description queries, it achieves

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:42.940
94.0% hit at 1, 97.4% hit at 9, and mean reciprocal rank of 0.9540.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:55.040
Re-ranking raises hit at 1, from 71.8% to 94.0%, median query processing time is 13.37 seconds with six concurrent queries.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:06.180
The benchmark supplies 45,064 labeled entries for evaluating LLM performance in systematic review screening across 32 curated secondary studies.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:11.880
and evaluation framework accounts for class imbalance between excluded and included articles.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:20.080
PromptSR supports prompt experimentation, experiment management, and result analysis for LLM-based screening.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:25.920
In QuestoScore, measures voice agent reliability as the percentage of calls in a fixed,

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:31.240
versioned evaluation population that achieved the colors goal without functional failure.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:37.020
It defines explicit failure events and severity levels, measures timing failures directly

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:42.860
from audio, and evaluates semantic failures with scenario predicates, tool traces, and

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.540
a pinned, open model judge.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:52.440
Version 0.1 evaluates 30 scenarios, three acoustic conditions, four speaker groups, and 306

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:55.120
calls per agent across 13 configurations.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:07:01.240
The synthetic data pipeline and self-improvement loop described earlier have been productionized

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:06.500
at Spotify and significantly accelerated iteration cycles for the launch of a conversational

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:07.720
recommendation agent.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:13.500
Try applying the sparse probing protocol from the BERT detection paper to your own frozen

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:19.840
encoder on a small labeled set to test whether a 1% neuron subset retains detection accuracy.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:25.660
run the cartograph federated proxy against an MCP server catalog to measure

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:30.960
token savings versus full catalog traversal on your own toolset. Test sign

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.160
trace on movement descriptions from your local sign language learners to see

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.800
whether the seven channel retrieval plus re-ranking reaches the reported

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:44.620
94% hit at one. Additional leave one family out results on the raid benchmark

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:49.420
are expected in follow-up work from the same lab. Production metrics from wider

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:54.980
deployment of the Spotify conversational agent will be reported in an upcoming industry case study.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:59.840
Expanded user trials for sign trace are planned with independent movement descriptions

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:05.740
collected from learners. Pop the hood on low-ranked bottleneck autoencoders and you see they replace

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:11.360
attention's dynamic weighting with a stack of fixed width modules that compress then reconstruct

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:17.860
token representations. Each module operates on local neighborhoods, the full sequence, or across

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:22.320
heads, with bottleneck width treated as a hyperparameter rather than learned.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:27.920
The iterative refinement step first averages and embedding with its neighbors, then projects

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:31.460
the result back onto the manifold learned by the autoencoder.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:37.280
On C4 pre-training, this approach reaches a substantial fraction of attention performance

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:42.840
while using roughly 1.9 times fewer flops than parameter matched burnt baselines.

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:48.520
lines. The frequency-aware masking schedule that over samples rare tokens allows the model

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:53.880
to match Bert and Tiny Bert on the lowest frequency bucket. When the bottleneck is too

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:59.580
narrow, reconstruction error rises sharply and downstream accuracy collapses. When it is

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:05.040
too wide, the flops advantage disappears. Teams should therefore sweep bottleneck width

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:10.360
on a small validation slice before scaling, and retain the frequency-aware schedule whenever

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:15.800
rare token performance matters. Before we go, watch for expanded user trials of

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:19.520
sign trace that will test the system on movement descriptions written

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.480
independently by learners rather than drawn from the dictionary. That's models

